  "worked_solution": "The denominators are $3$ and $9.$ We can make a common denominator of $9$ by splitting the shape on the left into $3$ parts vertically: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/641525dd15d4739bf548e5cde5e4fbff3b92e3eda3e7af46b0428b22bab1bad0.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{9}÷\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n3÷2=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{3}{\\boxed{\\,2\\,}}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $2.$",
